<section id="faqs">
<h2>❓ Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Q: My score is 75. Is that acceptable?</h3>
<p><strong>A:</strong> It depends. A lab or development forest at 75 is acceptable. A production forest should aim for 85+. A regulated environment (finance, healthcare) should target 90+. Also check your severity distribution—75 with 0 CRITICAL findings is much safer than 75 with 5 CRITICAL findings.</p>

<h3>Q: Why does fixing one issue only improve my score by 0.5%?</h3>
<p><strong>A:</strong> Your score is a weighted average across 111 checks. Each high-weight check is ~1–2% of your total. Fixing a low-weight check has minimal impact on the score, but may still be important for security. Don't optimize for the score number—optimize for reducing real risk.</p>

<h3>Q: The roadmap says to fix ACL-007, but it's "surgical." Should I skip it?</h3>
<p><strong>A:</strong> <strong>No.</strong> Don't skip it. ACL-007 is CRITICAL and affects your entire forest. Schedule dedicated time (2–4 weeks) with experienced admins, test in a non-prod environment, and implement with change control. Don't let "surgical" be an excuse to defer high-risk work indefinitely.</p>

<h3>Q: I have 200 stale accounts. How do I know which to remediate first?</h3>
<p><strong>A:</strong> Expand the ACCT-025 finding in the Findings Explorer. You'll see the full list of affected accounts. Prioritize by:</p>
<ol style="margin: 12px 0 12px 20px;">
  <li>Service accounts with privileges</li>
  <li>Accounts with high-risk permissions</li>
  <li>Accounts that haven't logged in in 2+ years</li>
  <li>Finally, regular user accounts in normal groups</li>
</ol>

<h3>Q: Can I customize the severity levels or weights?</h3>
<p><strong>A:</strong> Not yet through the UI, but check the configuration files in <code>config/</code>. Some exclusion lists and thresholds are configurable. For deeper customization, reach out to the strata team or file an issue.</p>

<h3>Q: What does "Affected: 0" mean on a finding?</h3>
<p><strong>A:</strong> It means the check <em>passed</em>. Only failing checks populate the affected objects list. If you see a passing check on the roadmap, it's informational or part of the trend analysis.</p>

